constructing examples of Mackey functors -- info about how to construct explicit Mackey functors
************************************************************************************************

The general constructor: New Mackey functors can be built using *note
makeCpMackeyFunctor: makeCpMackeyFunctor,, which constructs a Mackey functor
out of a restriction, transfer, and conjugation *note matrix:
(Macaulay2Doc)matrix,, in that order. It takes as input a prime, and three
matrices. For example, we can construct $\mathbb{F}_4$ as a $C_2$-Galois Mackey
functor as follows:



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : U:=cokernel(matrix({{2,0},{0,2}}));                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : C:=inducedMap(U,U,matrix({{1,1},{0,1}}));                               |
|                                                                             |
|o2 : Matrix                                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : F:=kernel(C - id_U);                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : R:=inducedMap(U,F,matrix({{1,0},{0,1}}));                               |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : Matrix                                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : T:=inducedMap(F,U,matrix({{0,1},{0,0}}));                               |
|                                                                             |
|o5 : Matrix                                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : M:=makeCpMackeyFunctor(2,R,T,C)                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o6 =                                   Res : | 1 0 |                         |
|                                             | 0 0 |                         |
|     subquotient (| 1 0 |, | 2 0 |)  -----------------> cokernel | 2 0 |  -┐ |
|                  | 0 2 |  | 0 2 |  <-----------------           | 0 2 |  <┘ |
|                                       Tr : | 0 1 |                          |
|                                            | 0 0 |                          |
|                                                                             |
|o6 : CpMackeyFunctor                                                         |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Conj : | 1 1 |                                                               |
|       | 0 1 |                                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The output of the *note makeCpMackeyFunctor: makeCpMackeyFunctor, method is a
*note CpMackeyFunctor: CpMackeyFunctor,, which is a new *note type:
(Macaulay2Doc)Type, implemented in this package. Under the hood it is a *note
hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)hash tables,, encoding all the data of the Mackey
functor. The underlying and fixed modules can be recovered with the *note
Underlying: Underlying, and *note Fixed: Fixed, keys, and the homomorphisms in
the data can be recovered from *note Res: Res,, *note Trans: Trans,, and *note
Conj: Conj, keys.



Pruning: Any time we might need a nicer, more readable form of a Mackey
functor, we can use the *note prune: prune_lpCpMackeyFunctor_rp, method to
simplify the presentation of the underlying/fixed modules.

Algebraic constructors: We provide specific methods for constructing common
examples of $C_p$-Mackey functors coming from algebra and representation
theory. The *note Burnside Mackey functor: makeBurnsideMackeyFunctor, is a
prototypical example -- its underlying module is $\ZZ$ with trivial conjugation
action, and its fixed module is the Burnside ring $A(C_p)$, which is the group
completion of the monoid of isomorphism classes of finite $C_p$-sets.

Another example from algebra are the *note real:
makeRealRepresentationMackeyFunctor, and *note complex representation:
makeComplexRepresentationMackeyFunctor, Mackey functors. Similarly to the
Burnside Mackey functor, their underlying module is $\ZZ$, however their fixed
module is the representation ring of $C_p$. Any finite $C_p$-set has an
associated permutation representation, which induces what are called the *note
real: realLinearizationMap, and *note complex linearization maps:
complexLinearizationMap, from the Burnside Mackey functor to the representation
Mackey functor.

Fixed point and orbit Mackey functors: If $M$ is a $\ZZ[C_p]$-module, the *note
fixed point Mackey functor: makeFixedPointMackeyFunctor, of $M$ has $M$ as its
underlying module and the fixed points $M^{C_p}$ as its fixed module. The
conjugation action on $M$ is the $C_p$-action, and the transfer sums along the
orbits of an element.



+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : makeFixedPointMackeyFunctor(2,matrix{{0,1,0},{1,0,0},{0,0,1}})|
|                                                                   |
|o7 =                  Res : | 1 0 |                                |
|                            | 1 0 |                                |
|                            | 0 1 |                                |
|     image | 1 0 |  ------------------->   3  -┐ Conj : | 0 1 0 |  |
|           | 1 0 | <-------------------  ZZ   <┘        | 1 0 0 |  |
|           | 0 1 |    Tr : | 1 1 0 |                    | 0 0 1 |  |
|                           | 0 0 2 |                               |
|                                                                   |
|o7 : CpMackeyFunctor                                               |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+

Similarly, given a $\ZZ[C_p]$-module $M$, we can form the *note orbit Mackey
functor: makeOrbitMackeyFunctor,, whose underlying module is again $M$ with
$C_p$-action yielding conjugation. The fixed point module is now the quotient
module $M/C_p$, transfer is the quotient map, and now restriction is defined by
summing along the orbits.



+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : makeOrbitMackeyFunctor(2,matrix{{0,1,0},{1,0,0},{0,0,1}})          |
|                                                                        |
|o8 =                         Res : | 1 1 0 |                            |
|                                   | 1 1 0 |                            |
|                                   | 0 0 2 |                            |
|     cokernel | 1  -1 0 |  ------------------->   3  -┐ Conj : | 0 1 0 ||
|              | -1 1  0 | <-------------------  ZZ   <┘        | 1 0 0 ||
|              | 0  0  0 |    Tr : | 1 1 0 |                    | 0 0 1 ||
|                                  | 0 0 0 |                             |
|                                  | 0 0 1 |                             |
|                                                                        |
|o8 : CpMackeyFunctor                                                    |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Free constructors: Analogous to how a free module can be constructed on a
generator or set of generators, we can construct free Mackey functors. For
$C_p$-Mackey functors, there are two modules, which lead to two different ideas
of a "free" Mackey functor, namely a free $C_p$-Mackey functor on a generator
in the underlying module, and a free $C_p$-Mackey functor on a generator in the
fixed module. The *note free Mackey functor on a single underlying generator:
makeUnderlyingFreeMackeyFunctor, can be accessed as follows:



+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : makeUnderlyingFreeMackeyFunctor(3)                |
|                                                       |
|o9 =        Res : | 1 |                                |
|                  | 1 |                                |
|                  | 1 |                                |
|       1  ------------------->   3  -┐ Conj : | 0 0 1 ||
|     ZZ  <-------------------  ZZ   <┘        | 1 0 0 ||
|            Tr : | 1 1 1 |                    | 0 1 0 ||
|                                                       |
|o9 : CpMackeyFunctor                                   |
+-------------------------------------------------------+

We often call this $\underline{B}$. Trying to construct a free Mackey functor
on a single generator in the fixed module, we can verify that it recovers a
similar construction, namely the *note Burnside Mackey functor:
makeBurnsideMackeyFunctor,, which we often denote by $\underline{A}$.



+--------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : makeBurnsideMackeyFunctor(5)                |
|                                                  |
|o10 =        Res : | 1 5 |                        |
|        2  ----------------->   1  -┐ Conj : | 1 ||
|      ZZ  <-----------------  ZZ   <┘             |
|             Tr : | 0 |                           |
|                  | 1 |                           |
|                                                  |
|o10 : CpMackeyFunctor                             |
+--------------------------------------------------+

These two Mackey functors $\underline{A}$ and $\underline{B}$ are very special
- they are the projective generators of the category $\text{Mack}_G$, and they
play an important role in *note constructing resolutions:
freeResolution_lpCpMackeyFunctor_cmZZ_rp,.

Random constructor: Furthermore we have a random constructor which allow us to
build a *note random Mackey functor: makeRandomCpMackeyFunctor, over the group
$C_p$. To implement this, we recall that we have projective generators
$\underline{A}$ and $\underline{B}$, hence any Mackey functor $M$ can be
written as a cokernel of some *note map:
map_lpCpMackeyFunctor_cmCpMackeyFunctor_cmMatrix_cmMatrix_rp, of the form

\[ \underline{A}^k \oplus \underline{B}^\ell \to \underline{A}^n \oplus
\underline{B}^m \to M \to 0.\]

We can specify $k$, $\ell$, $n$, and $m$ if we want, as the following example
over $C_7$ shows:



+--------------------------------------------+
|i11 : makeRandomCpMackeyFunctor(7,{2,2,3,3})|
|                                            |
|o11 =      Res : 0                          |
|      0  -----------> 0  -┐ Conj : 0        |
|        <-----------     <┘                 |
|           Tr : 0                           |
|                                            |
|o11 : CpMackeyFunctor                       |
+--------------------------------------------+

If we don't want to specify this data, $n$ and $m$ will be chosen randomly
between 1 and the value of the GenBound option (default 5). To randomly choose
between 1 and a higher number, set GenBound to that value.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : makeRandomCpMackeyFunctor(3)                                           |
|                                                                             |
|o12 =                    Res : | 3 -5  6 |                                   |
|                               | 0 333 3 |                                   |
|      cokernel | 10 |  ---------------------> cokernel | 15 |  -┐ Conj : | 1 |
|               | 0  | <---------------------           | 0  |  <┘        | 0 |
|               | 0  |    Tr : | -4 3 |                                       |
|                              | 0  0 |                                       |
|                              | 0  1 |                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o12 : CpMackeyFunctor                                                        |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|5 |                                                                          |
|1 |                                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : prune makeRandomCpMackeyFunctor(3,GenBound=>20)                        |
|                                                                             |
|o13 =      Res : 0                                                           |
|      0  -----------> 0  -┐ Conj : 0                                         |
|        <-----------     <┘                                                  |
|           Tr : 0                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o13 : CpMackeyFunctor                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Remark: What makes this possible is the fact that it is computationally very
easy to cook up a random map $\underline{A}^k \oplus \underline{B}^\ell \to
\underline{A}^n \oplus \underline{B}^m$ using the universal properties of the
functors $\underline{A}$ and $\underline{B}$. Since $\underline{A}$
corepresents the *note fixed module: Fixed, functor and $\underline{B}$
corepresents the *note underlying module: Underlying, functor, we can determine
random maps of the form $\underline{A}^k \oplus \underline{B}^\ell \to M$ by
picking a *note random list of elements:
(Macaulay2Doc)random_lpList_cmModule_rp, from the fixed and underlying modules
of any $M$.

