CpMackeyFunctor -- The type of Cp Mackey functors
*************************************************

Description
===========

This documents the *note type: (Macaulay2Doc)Type, of $C_p$-Mackey functors. A
$C_p$-Mackey functor is encoded as a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,
with keys through which we can pull various parts of the data.

The keys available (internally) in the hash table are as follows, together with
their external get methods:

  * .PrimeOrder yields the *note order: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ, of the cyclic group
    over which we're working. See *note PrimeOrder: PrimeOrder,
  * .Underlying encodes the *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)module, $M(C_p/e)$. See
    *note Underlying: Underlying,
  * .Fixed encodes the *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)module, $M(C_p/C_p)$. See
    *note Fixed: Fixed,
  * .Res encodes the restriction *note homomorphism: (Macaulay2Doc)module
    homomorphisms, $M(C_p/C_p) \to M(C_p/e)$. See *note Res: Res,
  * .Trans encodes the transfer *note homomorphism: (Macaulay2Doc)module
    homomorphisms, $M(C_p/e) \to M(C_p/C_p)$. See *note Trans: Trans,
  * .Conj encodes the conjugation *note homomorphism: (Macaulay2Doc)module
    homomorphisms, $M(C_p/e) \to M(C_p/e)$. See *note Conj: Conj,

Constructing new Mackey functors: See *note constructing examples of Mackey
functors: constructing examples of Mackey functors,.

Operations with Mackey functor types: One of the first operations to know about
is *note pruning: prune_lpCpMackeyFunctor_rp, a $C_p$-Mackey functor. This
basically takes the data of a Mackey functor and attempts to simplify it and
make it more reader-friendly. Pruning is the only unary operation on
$C_p$-Mackey functors that we consider. The unary (*note Boolean:
(Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,-valued) operations are

  * *note isTrivialMackeyFunctor: isTrivialMackeyFunctor, checks if a Mackey
    functor is trivial
  * *note isCohomological: isCohomological, checks if a Mackey functor is
    cohomological

For binary (and $n$-ary) operations, there are a few, including:

  * *note equality: (Macaulay2Doc)==, of two Mackey functors, via M==N
  * the *note box product: boxProduct, of two Mackey functors, via  M**N
  * the *note direct sum: (Macaulay2Doc)directSum, of two Mackey functors, as
    M++N
  * the *note internal hom: internalHom, of two Mackey functors

Well-definedness: The global variable *note assertLevel: assertLevel, controls
whether the methods that construct Mackey functors will check whether the input
data is well-defined. If *note assertLevel: assertLevel, is set to 0 or lower,
then no checks are made, and the methods will return a Mackey functor (which
may be malformed) regardless of the input data. If *note assertLevel:
assertLevel, is set to 1 or higher, then the methods will check whether the
input data yields a well-defined Mackey functor, and if not, they will throw an
error.

