the abelian category of Mackey functors -- background on the category of Mackey functors and how to code within it
******************************************************************************************************************

The category of Mackey functors: Fixing a finite group $G$ (always cyclic of
prime order in this package), we obtain a category $\text{Mack}_G$, whose
objects are *note Mackey functors: CpMackeyFunctor, and whose morphisms are
*note Mackey functor homomorphisms: MackeyFunctorHomomorphism,. Given any
Mackey functor $M$ we can take its identity homomorphism as follows:



+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : M = makeRandomCpMackeyFunctor(3,GenBound=>2);     |
+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : id_M                                              |
|                                                       |
|o2 =                     fix : | 1 |                   |
|       cokernel | 6 | <---------------   cokernel | 6 ||
|            ^ |                               ^ |      |
|            | |                               | |      |
|            | v                               | v      |
|     cokernel | 126 | <--------------- cokernel | 126 ||
|            ^ |          und : | 1 |          ^ |      |
|            └-┘                               └-┘      |
|                                                       |
|o2 : MackeyFunctorHomomorphism                         |
+-------------------------------------------------------+

*note Composition: MackeyFunctorHomomorphism _st MackeyFunctorHomomorphism,  of
Mackey functor homomorphisms is done exactly the same as for composition of
maps. If $f\colon M \to N$ and $g \colon N \to Q$ are two Mackey functor
homomorphisms, their composite $g\circ f \colon M \to Q$ can be accessed as
g*f.



The abelian category structure: Given any two Mackey functors $M$ and $N$, we
can form the set $\text{Hom}_{\text{Mack}_G}(M,N)$ of homomorphisms from $M$ to
$N$. Since we can *note add: MackeyFunctorHomomorphism, any two homomorphisms,
we have that $\text{Hom}_{\text{Mack}_G}(M,N)$ is really an *note abelian group
of homomorphisms: Hom_lpCpMackeyFunctor_cmCpMackeyFunctor_rp,. Composition is
moreover $\ZZ$-linear, hence we can say that the category $\text{Mack}_G$ is
pre-additive.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : Hom(makeRandomCpMackeyFunctor(2,GenBound=>2),makeRandomCpMackeyFunctor(2|
|                                                                             |
|o3 = 0                                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : ZZ-module                                                               |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|,GenBound=>2))                                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : prune oo                                                                |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = 0                                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : ZZ-module                                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Given any two Mackey functors, we can take their *note direct sum:
(Macaulay2Doc)directSum, and obtain another Mackey functor. This is a finite
biproduct on the category $\text{Mack}_G$, in other words it is both a product
and a coproduct. There is a zero object for this biproduct, namely the *note
zero Mackey functor: makeZeroMackeyFunctor,. This gives $\text{Mack}_G$ what is
often called a semiadditive structure.

Moreover, any Mackey functor homomorphism admits a *note kernel:
kernel_lpMackeyFunctorHomomorphism_rp, and a *note cokernel:
cokernel_lpMackeyFunctorHomomorphism_rp,. All this data we've collected so far
is often called a pre-abelian structure. Finally, one can prove that images and
coimages agree, hence $\text{Mack}_G$ is an abelian category.

Enough projectives: The category $\text{Mack}_{G}$ has a set of two projective
generators, which we denote by $\underline{A}$ and $\underline{B}$. These are
the *note Burnside Mackey functor: makeBurnsideMackeyFunctor, and the *note
underlying free Mackey functor: makeUnderlyingFreeMackeyFunctor,, respectively.
In particular, the category $\text{Mack}_G$ has enough projectives, meaning in
particular that we can take projective resolutions in order to compute ext and
tor groups.

Free resolutions: Given any Mackey functor we can take its free resolution. The
projective dimension of an object of $\text{Mack}_G$ is generally infinite, so
we cannot provide all of the data of a resolution -- nevertheless we can
provide a computation that holds in a range. This is accomplished using the
*note freeResolution: (Complexes)freeResolution, (aka *note freeResolution:
(Complexes)freeResolution,) method, which inputs a Mackey functor $M$ and an
integer $n$, and outputs a free resolution of the form \[M \leftarrow F_0
\leftarrow F_1 \leftarrow \cdots \leftarrow F_n\]



+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : M = prune makeRandomCpMackeyFunctor(3,GenBound=>2);|
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : class res(M,2)                                     |
|                                                        |
|o6 = List                                               |
|                                                        |
|o6 : Type                                               |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : length resolution(M,2)                             |
|                                                        |
|o7 = 3                                                  |
+--------------------------------------------------------+

In the notation above, the element res(M,2) will be a *note list:
(Macaulay2Doc)List, of differentials $(M \leftarrow F_0, F_0 \leftarrow F_1,
F_1 \leftarrow F_2)$, where each entry in the list is a *note Mackey functor
homomorphism: MackeyFunctorHomomorphism,.

When a resolution is computed, this is *note cached: (Macaulay2Doc)cache, in
the Mackey functor itself, and can be accessed as M.ProjRes. This makes
computations less costly, and moreover the cache helps us expand resolutions.
For instance if we have already computed res(M,10) and want to compute
res(M,20), it will access the cached 10-term resolution to shorten the
computation for the 20-term resolution.

The monoidal structure: The category $\text{Mack}_G$ furthermore has a
symmetric monoidal structure, given by the *note box product: boxProduct, of
two Mackey functors. Taking the box product admits a right adjoint, in other
words there is an *note internal hom: internalHom,, which is a functor

\[\underline{\text{Hom}} \colon \text{Mack}_G^{\text{op}} \times \text{Mack}_G
\to \text{Mack}_G,\]

whose *note fixed point module: Fixed, is the *note abelian group of
homomorphisms: Hom_lpCpMackeyFunctor_cmCpMackeyFunctor_rp, from $M$ to $N$. The
category $\text{Mack}_G$ is closed symmetric monoidal, meaning the internal hom
and box product fit together into a natural isomorphism of abelian groups,
natural in any three Mackey functors $M$, $N$, and $P$:
\[\text{Hom}_{\text{Mack}_G}(M \square N, P) \cong
\text{Hom}_{\text{Mack}_G}(M, \underline{\text{Hom}}(N,P)).\]





Ext and Tor: With free resolutions in mind, we can define internal *note ext:
Ext, and *note tor: Tor, groups as the derived functors of the internal hom and
box product, respectively:

\[\underline{\text{Ext}}^i(-,M):= \mathbf{R}^i
\underline{\text{Hom}}_{\text{Mack}_G}(-,M),\]

\[\underline{\text{Tor}}^i(-,M) := \mathbf{L}_i (- \square M).\]

These are internal incarnations of $\text{Ext}$ and $\text{Tor}$. We can
recover the abelian groups $\text{Ext}^i(M,N)$ and $\text{Tor}_i(M,N)$ as the
*note fixed point module: Fixed, of the internal Ext and Tor.



See also
========

  * *note background on Mackey functors: background on Mackey functors, -- a
    brief mathematical intro to the theory of Mackey functors
  * *note constructing examples of Mackey functors: constructing examples of
    Mackey functors, -- info about how to construct explicit Mackey functors
  * *note list of common Mackey functors: list of common Mackey functors, -- a
    list of common Cp-Mackey functors
  * *note explicit applications of the CpMackeyFunctors package: explicit
    applications of the CpMackeyFunctors package, -- info about how this
    package can be applied to real-life examples
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

